  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1221 AM CDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nToday - Monday: Dry and Seasonable\n\nNorthwest flow continues through Monday as a trough sits off to our \neast and a ridge to our west. This ridge decays as it moves eastward \nas we go through Monday. With the ridge axis over the central CONUS \ntoday and Monday dry conditions continue and high temperatures \nremain slightly cooler than normal in the mid 70s to low 80s. \nDewpoints also remain on the lower side, in the 40s and 50s. The \nonly forecast concern over the next couple of days is some fog. \nThrough this morning it looks to be confined mainly to Mississippi \nRiver tributaries. Heading into tonight/early Monday morning, the \nwinds shift to the east southeast which could help the fog potential \nbecome more widespread, depending on how when upper level clouds \nmove in. Overall though, a really nice couple of days are in store \nfor us!\n\nTuesday - Wednesday: Storm Potential\n\nCircling back to the current conditions, a trough just moved onshore \nin the Pacific Northwest. This trough will ride around the ridge and \neventually move over the Upper Midwest for Tuesday into Wednesday. \nForecast soundings from the deterministic GFS and ECMWF show some \nlow-level curvature and elongation with their hodographs. This is \nslightly different from previous soundings from this morning which \nhighlighted more elongation with little curvature. The GFS and ECMWF \nboth have quite a bit of DCAPE (around 1000 to 1200 J/kg) and modest \n0-6km shear (35 to 50 kts). All this to say that if storms can \ninitiate then these parameters would support severe convection. The \none caveat to this forecast is that we are still 3 days out from \nthis event, so there are quite a bit of details to iron out still so \nstay tuned for further updates on this storm potential.\n\nThursday - Sunday: Dry Early with Increased Storm Potential for the \nWeekend\n\nWith the trough exiting the region on Wednesday, northwest flow \nreturns for Thursday and Friday resulting in mostly dry conditions. \nThe flow then shifts to a more quasi zonal/northwest flow pattern. \nPrecipitation chances look to increase as we head into the weekend \nas a couple of shortwaves pass through the region. There is still \nuncertainty given we are this far out from these waves impacting the \nregion, however a few ensemble members of the GEFS and EPS due show \nsome potential (10%) of 1 to 2\" of rain possible.",
